.TH GPUTEST 1
.SH NAME
gputest \- test gpu(3)'s handle table and gpu(2)'s precision negotiation
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gputest
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Gputest
exercises two things
.IR gpubench (1)
cannot: that
.IR gpu (3)
keeps many handles apart under concurrent use, and that
.IR gpu (2)
refuses to hand back single precision to a caller who asked for double.
.PP
The first check builds a diagonal
.IR sparse (2)
matrix and applies it twice through
.IR gpu (2),
once with
.B "precision f64"
and once with
.BR f32 .
The f64 attempt must be
.B declined
\(em this device is f32 on every path (see
.IR gpu (3))
\(em and the f32 attempt must both succeed and compute the right answer.
.PP
The second check spawns forty processes, each of which opens its own
.BR /dev/gpuclone ,
uploads a differently-sized diagonal matrix, and performs sixty matvecs
against it, verifying every element each time.
Forty is chosen to be larger than the sixteen-entry quantum
.I gpu (3)'s
handle table grows by, so the table is reallocated while other processes
are already using handles out of it.
The matrices are diagonal with small integer entries, so every expected
value is exact even after the f32 wire round trip, and any wrong value
means the device has confused one handle with another rather than merely
lost precision.
.PP
It talks to
.B /dev/gpuclone
directly rather than through
.IR gpu (2)
for that second part, deliberately:
.IR gpu (2)
holds its device descriptor and matrix order in module-level variables
and documents itself as not reentrant, so driving it from many processes
at once would exercise the wrong thing.
What has to survive many handles at once is the device.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/gputest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR gpu (2),
.IR gpu (3),
.IR gpubench (1),
.IR sparse (2)
.SH BUGS
Under
.B emu-g
no hardware backend is linked, so the matvecs run on
.IR gpu (3)'s
own portable CPU loop and the test covers the protocol and handle
lifecycle but not Metal.
.PP
It is meant to be run under
.B emu-cocoa
as well, to cover the Metal path \(em but there it currently fails
almost every run, and not for any reason of its own.
The fault is a segmentation violation inside
.B dopoolalloc
reached from
.BR kstrdup / kproc
in
.BR release ,
called from
.BR Sys_open :
heap corruption in the allocator under concurrency, on a build with no
.I gpu
changes at all.
See the open-bug section of
.B /doc/hpc-plan.md
before spending time on it, and do not read a failure there as a fault
of this test or of
.IR gpu (3).
.PP
It does not test
.BR resident ,
nor uploading a second matrix over a live handle, nor any matrix that is
not diagonal \(em
.IR gpubench (1)
covers real matrices, on one handle.
.PP
This test is heavily concurrent and so is itself vulnerable to the
scheduler hang described in
.IR fdstresstest (1):
roughly one run in ten produces no output and never exits, which is that
bug and not this one.
Run it under an external timeout and count a timeout separately from a
failure, or the hang will swamp whatever you were trying to measure \(em
it masked this test's own signal completely until the two were separated.
